coverage-floor.remediation:
Add tests for the code the change introduced, then set the floor to the coverage the build already reaches, so it can only go up from there:

```json
{
  "guardrails": {
    "checks": [
      { "use": "tests/coverage-floor@v1", "with": { "minLines": "72" } }
    ]
  }
}
```

The guardrail needs a coverage report in the workspace, which most builds produce with a separate task:

```bash
./gradlew test jacocoTestReport
```

Both floors default to `0`, so a build that hasn't chosen a number is never failed by one. Raise them as coverage improves. A floor set above where the build is today fails every change until someone lowers it, and that teaches the team to ignore it.

no-skipped-growth.remediation:
Re-enable the skipped tests, or delete the ones that are never coming back. A skipped test still costs as much to maintain and tells you nothing.

If a skip is deliberate, set the budget to the number you accept, and lower it as tests are re-enabled:

```json
{
  "guardrails": {
    "checks": [
      { "use": "tests/no-skipped-growth@v1", "with": { "maxSkipped": "3" } }
    ]
  }
}
```

This guardrail compares against `maxSkipped` and nothing else. It doesn't look at the previous build, so the number in the configuration is what holds the line, and lowering it is a commit someone reviews.
